For anyone reading through this thread, and interested in how we fitted the mentioned African Swine Fever spatial spread model, a pre-proof version of the paper is now available online https://doi.org/10.1016/j.epidem.2022.100596

Cheers
David

> One alternative to help overcome the first problem is to switch to a stochastic model. Using something like the tau-leap method you can track when individuals enter and leave each compartment. We did this recently in a spatial SIR model for African Swine Fever spread among wild boar, and used 10-day aggregates of observed cases (in each pixel) in our likelihood calculations (we used a Poisson likelihood, with the expected value obtained from Monte Carlo simulation). Here's a temporary link to the paper <https://we.tl/t-YTasoc83UF>, which has just been accepted for publication in a special issue of Epidemics. Normally you should be able to access our code via the links in the paper. Feel free to shoot me a message if you need some pointers navigating the code to the parts which are relevant to you.
